    {
      "id": "4GPzkGBGOLX5rV_NWYoHx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 989.69921875,
      "y": 71.41796875,
      "width": 746.8994750976562,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2122889628,
      "version": 15,
      "versionNonce": 1961232086,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Requirements\n* The component should be generic enough to be usable by different\nwebsites.\n* The input field UI and search results UI should be customizable.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Requirements\n* The component should be generic enough to be usable by different websites.\n* The input field UI and search results UI should be customizable.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ghuKePU2wE8sH080E5ZB9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 995.4164565556625,
      "y": 177.5,
      "width": 1032.69921875,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 903068444,
      "version": 264,
      "versionNonce": 964884874,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Questions\n1. What kind of results should be support (text, image, media/media with text)\n2. What devices will this component used (laptio/tablets/mobile)\n3. support fuzzy search \n(search technique that finds text matches even when the user query does not perfectly match the data)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Questions\n1. What kind of results should be support (text, image, media/media with text)\n2. What devices will this component used (laptio/tablets/mobile)\n3. support fuzzy search \n(search technique that finds text matches even when the user query does not perfectly match the data)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2hVrHIvCfECXyuMLiAh_e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 980.6290856836317,
      "y": 335.3770299980773,
      "width": 1796.31884765625,
      "height": 2250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aX",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 800002460,
      "version": 5404,
      "versionNonce": 1332395030,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Interface definition (API)\n* number of results\n* API URL /search?query=<>\n* Event listener: input/focus/blur/change/select => document.eventListener, adding DOM hook/input hook to these event\n\n--- API ----\n* Minimum query length: trigger search only when minimum number of characters have been typed in (reduce irrelevant results)\n* Debounce duration:\nTriggering a back end search API for every keystroke can be quite wasteful, \nespecially when the queries for the first few characters are likely to not be meaningful. \nDebouncing is a technique that limits the number of times a function gets called. \nWe could debounce the API calls so that the server does not get hit too often. With a debounce duration of 300ms, \nthe back end search API will only be called after there has been no user input for 300ms\n* API timeout: how long we should wait for response before determine if search has time out\n* Cache-related\n   * initial results\n   * result source: network only/network and cache/cache only\n   * Function to merge result from server and cache\n   * cache durration\n* Server API: \n   * query/limit/pagination => useful if there is a need to allow users to scroll down beyond the initial list\n\n* Optimization\n* Network\n    * user makes changes to the query while there's a pending network request? \n      If there are multiple pending network requests, we will need to be mindful not to display results for a previous search query\n    * We cannot rely on return order of network responses from server because earlier request might complete later than request fired after\n    * There are 2 ways to know which requests's response we should diplay\n       1. Attach timestamp for reach request, only display the result of latest request (not latest response). \n       2. Save the results in object/map, keyed by query string  ==> option 2 is better for storing caching response for each query, more deterministic\n(Not advisable to abort request/Abortcontroller or discard response since server would received and processed the request and return the data back to the client)\n* Failed request/retries\n     * Server request can sometimes fail, due to flaky internet connection\n     * Component can automatically retry firing the query. In case server is indeed offline, and we concern about overloading it, we could use exponential backoff strategy\n* OFffline usage\n     * If device entirely lost its network connection, there's not a lot we could do. \n     * Read purely from cache (not useful if cache empty)\n     * Not fire any request so not waste an CPU cycle.\n     * UX: indicate in component somewhere there's no network connection.\n--------------------------------------------\n* Cache: O(1) to retrieve result. \nOption1: good for short lived website, even though there is duplicated data, user unlikely to search so often that memory can become issues\nCache is cleared/reset when user click on specific search result\nconst cache = {\n    fa: [\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'Facebook'},\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'FasTrak'},\n      {type: 'text', text: 'family'...}\n    ],\n};\n\nOption2: more long live application, however, caching too long might be a bad idea as stale results take memory without being useful\nto save space, we could also normalize the cache\nconst results: [\n   {id: 1, text: 'Facebook', ...},\n   {id: 2: text: 'Fastrack', ...}\n],\nconst cache = {\n   fa: [1, 2, ...],\n   fac: [1, 2, 3...]\n};\n\n==> Caching is space/time tradeoff, where memory space to save processing time. Dedepending type of application to determine the usefulness of cache (search vs. network vs. strock)\n\n* Performance -- refers to client side performance\n-- Loading speed: need to depends on how fast server returns response, but with caching on client side, we can show results for previous queries, can go a step further and\nuse cache results for future queries if there is match\n-- Debounce/throttling: limit number of network requests can be fired, reduce server load and CPU processing\n-- Memory usage: long-lived pages might have autocomplete component that acccumulate too many results, purging the cache and free up memory is essential here.\nThe purging can be done when the browser is idle, or when total memory/number of cache entires exceed certain threshold\nVirtualized list: only render the nodes that are visible and recycle the DOM nodes instead of creating a new ones\n\n\n* User experience\n-- Autofocus\n-- Handle different state (loading, error, no network)\n-- Handle long string/long text with wrap or ellipsis. (no text overflow outside component)\n-- Mobile friendly (line height, each item can be large enough to tap in mobile device, response fit in mobile view port)\n-- Set helpful attributes for mobile: autocapitalize=\"off\", autocomplete=\"off\", autocorrect=\"off\", \nand spellcheck=\"false\" so that the browser suggestions do not interfere with the user's search.\n-- Support keyboard interaction\n-- Where auto compleete response should appear (below/above), depending on the position of the search bar\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Interface definition (API)\n* number of results\n* API URL /search?query=<>\n* Event listener: input/focus/blur/change/select => document.eventListener, adding DOM hook/input hook to these event\n\n--- API ----\n* Minimum query length: trigger search only when minimum number of characters have been typed in (reduce irrelevant results)\n* Debounce duration:\nTriggering a back end search API for every keystroke can be quite wasteful, \nespecially when the queries for the first few characters are likely to not be meaningful. \nDebouncing is a technique that limits the number of times a function gets called. \nWe could debounce the API calls so that the server does not get hit too often. With a debounce duration of 300ms, \nthe back end search API will only be called after there has been no user input for 300ms\n* API timeout: how long we should wait for response before determine if search has time out\n* Cache-related\n   * initial results\n   * result source: network only/network and cache/cache only\n   * Function to merge result from server and cache\n   * cache durration\n* Server API: \n   * query/limit/pagination => useful if there is a need to allow users to scroll down beyond the initial list\n\n* Optimization\n* Network\n    * user makes changes to the query while there's a pending network request? \n      If there are multiple pending network requests, we will need to be mindful not to display results for a previous search query\n    * We cannot rely on return order of network responses from server because earlier request might complete later than request fired after\n    * There are 2 ways to know which requests's response we should diplay\n       1. Attach timestamp for reach request, only display the result of latest request (not latest response). \n       2. Save the results in object/map, keyed by query string  ==> option 2 is better for storing caching response for each query, more deterministic\n(Not advisable to abort request/Abortcontroller or discard response since server would received and processed the request and return the data back to the client)\n* Failed request/retries\n     * Server request can sometimes fail, due to flaky internet connection\n     * Component can automatically retry firing the query. In case server is indeed offline, and we concern about overloading it, we could use exponential backoff strategy\n* OFffline usage\n     * If device entirely lost its network connection, there's not a lot we could do. \n     * Read purely from cache (not useful if cache empty)\n     * Not fire any request so not waste an CPU cycle.\n     * UX: indicate in component somewhere there's no network connection.\n--------------------------------------------\n* Cache: O(1) to retrieve result. \nOption1: good for short lived website, even though there is duplicated data, user unlikely to search so often that memory can become issues\nCache is cleared/reset when user click on specific search result\nconst cache = {\n    fa: [\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'Facebook'},\n      {type: 'organization', text: 'FasTrak'},\n      {type: 'text', text: 'family'...}\n    ],\n};\n\nOption2: more long live application, however, caching too long might be a bad idea as stale results take memory without being useful\nto save space, we could also normalize the cache\nconst results: [\n   {id: 1, text: 'Facebook', ...},\n   {id: 2: text: 'Fastrack', ...}\n],\nconst cache = {\n   fa: [1, 2, ...],\n   fac: [1, 2, 3...]\n};\n\n==> Caching is space/time tradeoff, where memory space to save processing time. Dedepending type of application to determine the usefulness of cache (search vs. network vs. strock)\n\n* Performance -- refers to client side performance\n-- Loading speed: need to depends on how fast server returns response, but with caching on client side, we can show results for previous queries, can go a step further and\nuse cache results for future queries if there is match\n-- Debounce/throttling: limit number of network requests can be fired, reduce server load and CPU processing\n-- Memory usage: long-lived pages might have autocomplete component that acccumulate too many results, purging the cache and free up memory is essential here.\nThe purging can be done when the browser is idle, or when total memory/number of cache entires exceed certain threshold\nVirtualized list: only render the nodes that are visible and recycle the DOM nodes instead of creating a new ones\n\n\n* User experience\n-- Autofocus\n-- Handle different state (loading, error, no network)\n-- Handle long string/long text with wrap or ellipsis. (no text overflow outside component)\n-- Mobile friendly (line height, each item can be large enough to tap in mobile device, response fit in mobile view port)\n-- Set helpful attributes for mobile: autocapitalize=\"off\", autocomplete=\"off\", autocorrect=\"off\", \nand spellcheck=\"false\" so that the browser suggestions do not interfere with the user's search.\n-- Support keyboard interaction\n-- Where auto compleete response should appear (below/above), depending on the position of the search bar\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "SUlgd-_QoZkA_s5OS9hOm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 270.3529685916866,
      "y": 758.7276739474231,
      "width": 631.0195922851562,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aa",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1904455223,
      "version": 1094,
      "versionNonce": 1052719178,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Data Model:\n* Controller\n    * props/options exposed via component API\n    * Current search string\n* Cache\n    * Initial results\n    * Cache results\n\n* Memory usage:\n   * Long lived pages might have autocomplete components\n that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such\npages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain\nthreshold\n* Virtual list\n * If the results contains many item, rendering that many\nDOM nodes in the browser woul cost lost of memory and slow\ndown the brpwser. List virtualization is technique can be used\nto help component retain performance at scale.\n\n* List virtualization, or \"windowing\", \nis the concept of only rendering what is visible to the user. \nThe number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\"\n of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering \nand scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Data Model:\n* Controller\n    * props/options exposed via component API\n    * Current search string\n* Cache\n    * Initial results\n    * Cache results\n\n* Memory usage:\n   * Long lived pages might have autocomplete components\n that accumulate too many results in the cache & hog memory\nPurging the cache and freeing up memory is essential for such\npages. The purging can be done when the browser is idle or when\nthe total memory/number of cache entries exceed a certain\nthreshold\n* Virtual list\n * If the results contains many item, rendering that many\nDOM nodes in the browser woul cost lost of memory and slow\ndown the brpwser. List virtualization is technique can be used\nto help component retain performance at scale.\n\n* List virtualization, or \"windowing\", \nis the concept of only rendering what is visible to the user. \nThe number of elements that are rendered at first is\n a very small subset of the entire list and the \"window\"\n of visible content moves when the user continues to scroll. \nThis improves both the rendering \nand scrolling performance of the list.\n\n\n ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "s7ua7V-RXEmWAPRDuw4Ss",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 494.5641576674347,
      "y": -319.94609205665824,
      "width": 1327.119140625,
      "height": 150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ag",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1214360697,
      "version": 709,
      "versionNonce": 2049362262,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Design interview: (RADIO)\n* Requirement exploration: understand the problem thoroughly and determine the scope by asking clarifying questions\n* Architecture/high level design: list out key components of products and how they relate to each other (always have high level design)\n* Data model/core entities: describe core entities and its data -- fields each entities contain/which component it belongs to\n* API: define API between component, functionality for each API, parameters and response\n* Optimization and deep-dive",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Design interview: (RADIO)\n* Requirement exploration: understand the problem thoroughly and determine the scope by asking clarifying questions\n* Architecture/high level design: list out key components of products and how they relate to each other (always have high level design)\n* Data model/core entities: describe core entities and its data -- fields each entities contain/which component it belongs to\n* API: define API between component, functionality for each API, parameters and response\n* Optimization and deep-dive",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BfyAN-c9SGLwRQhmXc8ua",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 255.45369203340454,
      "y": 1536.2375215986672,
      "width": 638.4794921875,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "an",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1261772710,
      "version": 307,
      "versionNonce": 931503882,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781548598986,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Fuzzy search:\n* handle typo in search, especially mobile device, use fuzzy search\nto match search query closely instead of exactly\n* Levenstain distance (find shortest distance) between\nsearch query and results\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Fuzzy search:\n* handle typo in search, especially mobile device, use fuzzy search\nto match search query closely instead of exactly\n* Levenstain distance (find shortest distance) between\nsearch query and results\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
